== God Glorified in Man’s Dependence

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(style: "italic")[#emph[1 Cor 1:29–31.] So that no flesh may glory in His presence. But it is of Him that you#super[+] are in Christ Jesus, who from God has become for us wisdom, and righteousness, and sanctification, and redemption; so that, as it is written: “Let him who glories glory in the Lord.”]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[Those] Christians to whom the apostle wrote this epistle lived in a part of the world where human wisdom was held in great esteem, as the apostle observes in the 22nd verse of this chapter: “Greeks search for wisdom.” #emph[Corinth] was not far from #emph[Athens,] which for many ages had been the most famous seat of philosophy and learning in the world.

So the apostle points out to them how God, by the gospel, destroyed their human wisdom and brought it “to nothing.” The learned Greeks and their great philosophers, with all their wisdom, did not know God; they were not able to find out the truth in divine things. But after they had done their utmost to no effect, it pleased God at last to reveal himself by the gospel, which they counted foolishness: “God chose the foolish things of the world to shame the wise; God chose the weak things of the world to shame the strong. He chose the lowly and despised things of the world, and the things that are not, to bring to nothing the things that are.” And the apostle tells them why he did this, in the verse of the text: “so that no flesh may glory in His presence,” and so on.

In these words we may observe:

1\. What God aims at in the way he has ordered things in the matter of redemption: that man should not glory in himself, but in God alone. “So that no flesh may glory in His presence … so that, as it is written: ‘Let him who glories glory in the Lord.’ ”

2\. How this end is reached in the work of redemption: by that absolute and immediate dependence which men have on God in that work for all their good. For:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] all the good that they have is in and through Christ. He “has become for us wisdom, righteousness, sanctification, and redemption.” All the good of the fallen and redeemed creature is contained in these four things, and cannot be better divided than into them. But Christ is each of them to us, and we have none of them in any other way than in him. “From God He has become for us wisdom”: in him is all the understanding’s own good and true excellence. Wisdom was something the Greeks prized; but Christ is the true light of the world, and it is through him alone that true wisdom is imparted to the mind. It is in and by Christ that we have #emph[righteousness]: it is by being in him that we are justified, have our sins pardoned, and are received as righteous into God’s favor. It is by Christ that we have #emph[sanctification]: in him we have true excellence of heart as well as of understanding, and he has become for us inherent as well as imputed righteousness. It is by Christ that we have #emph[redemption,] that is, actual deliverance from all misery and the bestowal of all happiness and glory. So we have all our good by Christ, who is God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] another instance in which our dependence on God for all our good appears is this: it is God who has given us Christ, so that we might have these benefits through him. He “from God has become for us wisdom, righteousness,” and so on.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] it is #emph[of him] that we are #emph[in Christ Jesus,] and come to have a share in him, and so receive those blessings which he has become for us. It is God who gives us the faith by which we embrace Christ.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So in this verse our dependence on each person of the Trinity for all our good is shown. We depend on Christ the Son of God, as he is our “wisdom, righteousness, sanctification, and redemption.” We depend on the Father, who has given us Christ and made him to be these things to us. We depend on the Holy Spirit, for it is #emph[of him that we are in Christ Jesus]\; it is the Spirit of God who gives faith in him, by which we receive him and embrace him.]

#emph[Doctrine.] God is glorified in the work of redemption in this: that in it there appears so absolute and universal a dependence of the redeemed on him.

Here I propose to show, 1. that there is an absolute and universal dependence of the redeemed on God for all their good; and 2. that God is exalted and glorified by this in the work of redemption.

I. There is an absolute and universal dependence of the redeemed on God. The nature and design of our redemption are such that the redeemed are in everything directly, immediately, and entirely dependent on God: they depend on him for all, and depend on him in every way.

The several ways in which one being may depend on another for its good, and in which the redeemed of Jesus Christ depend on God for all their good, are these: they have all their good #emph[of] him, they have all #emph[through] him, and they have all #emph[in] him. Insofar as he is the cause and source from which all their good comes, it is #emph[of] him. Insofar as he is the medium by which it is obtained and conveyed, they have it #emph[through] him. And insofar as he is that very good which is given and conveyed, it is #emph[in] him.

Now those who are redeemed by Jesus Christ depend on God in all these respects, very directly and entirely, for their all.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, the redeemed have all their good #emph[of] God. God is its great author. He is its first cause; and not only so, but he is its only true cause.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is #emph[of] God that we have our Redeemer. It is God who has provided a Savior for us. Jesus Christ is not only of God in his person, as he is the only begotten Son of God; he is also from God as he concerns us, and in his office of Mediator. He is God’s gift to us: God chose and anointed him, appointed him his work, and sent him into the world.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as it is God who gives the Savior, so it is God who accepts him. As it is God who provides and gives the Redeemer to buy salvation for us, so it is #emph[of] God that that salvation is bought: he gives the purchaser, and he provides the thing purchased.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is of God that Christ becomes ours, that we are brought to him and united to him. It is of God that we receive faith to embrace him, so that we may have a share in him: “For it is by grace you#super[+] have been saved through faith, and this not of yourselves#super[+]\; it is the gift of God” (Eph 2:8). It is of God that we actually receive all the benefits Christ has purchased. It is God who pardons and justifies, and delivers from going down to hell; and it is his favor that the redeemed are received into, and are made the objects of, when they are justified. So too it is God who delivers from the dominion of sin, cleanses us from our filthiness, and changes us from our deformity. It is of God that the redeemed receive all their true excellence, wisdom and holiness, and that in two ways. First, the Holy Spirit, by whom these things are directly worked, is from God: he proceeds from him and is sent by him. Second, the Holy Spirit himself is God, and by his working and indwelling the knowledge of God and divine things, a holy disposition, and all grace are conferred and upheld.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And though means are used in conferring grace on men’s souls, yet it is of God that we have these means of grace, and it is God who makes them effectual. It is of God that we have the holy Scriptures; they are the word of God. It is of God that we have ordinances, and their efficacy depends on the immediate influence of the Spirit of God. The ministers of the gospel are sent by God, and all their sufficiency is of him: “we have this treasure in jars of clay to show that this surpassingly great power is of God and not of us” (2 Cor 4:7). Their success depends entirely and absolutely on the immediate blessing and influence of God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The redeemed have all things:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Of the #emph[grace] of God. It was of sheer grace that God gave us his only begotten Son. The grace is great in proportion to the dignity and excellence of what is given. The gift was infinitely precious, because it was a person infinitely worthy, a person of infinite glory, and also because it was a person infinitely near and dear to God. The grace is great in proportion to the benefit given us in him. The benefit is doubly infinite, in that in him we have deliverance from an infinite misery (infinite because eternal), and also receive eternal joy and glory. The grace in bestowing this gift is great in proportion to our unworthiness to whom it is given: instead of deserving such a gift, we deserved infinitely ill at God’s hands. The grace is great according to the manner of giving, that is, in proportion to the humbling and cost of the method and means by which the way was made for us to have the gift. He gave him to us dwelling among us. He gave him to us incarnate, that is, in our own nature. He gave him to us in our nature with infirmities like those it has in us in our fallen state, which in us accompany, and are occasioned by, the sinful corruption of our nature. He gave him to us in a low and afflicted state; and not only so, but he gave him to us slain, so that he might be a feast for our souls.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The grace of God in bestowing this gift is most free. God was under no obligation to bestow it: he might have rejected fallen man, as he did the fallen angels. We never did anything to merit it: it was given while we were still enemies, and before we had so much as repented. It came from the love of God, which saw no excellence in us to attract it; and it was given without any expectation of ever being repaid for it.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And it is from sheer grace that the benefits of Christ are applied to particular persons, to this one and that one. Those who are called and sanctified are to attribute it to the good pleasure of God’s goodness alone, by which they are set apart. He is sovereign, and has mercy on whom he will have mercy, and hardens whom he will.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Man now has a greater dependence on the grace of God than he had before the fall. He depends on God’s free goodness for much more than he did then. Then he depended on God’s goodness to confer the reward of perfect obedience, for God was not obliged to promise and bestow that reward. But now we depend on the grace of God for much more. We need grace not only to bestow glory on us, but to deliver us from hell and eternal wrath. Under the first covenant we depended on God’s goodness to give us the reward of righteousness, and so we do now. And not only so, but we need God’s free and sovereign grace to give us that righteousness. And not only that, but we need his grace to pardon our sin and release us from its guilt and infinite demerit.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as we depend on the goodness of God for more now than under the first covenant, so we depend on a much greater, freer and more wonderful goodness. We are now more dependent on God’s unconstrained and sovereign good pleasure. In our first estate we depended on God for holiness: we had our original righteousness from him. But then holiness was not bestowed by sovereign good pleasure in the way it is now. Man was created holy, and it was fitting for God to create holy all the rational creatures he created. It would have been a disparagement of the holiness of God’s nature if he had made an intelligent creature unholy. But now, when man is made holy, it is from sheer and unconstrained grace. God may deny holiness to the fallen creature forever if he pleases, without any disparagement to any of his perfections.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And not only are we in fact more dependent on the grace of God, but our dependence is much more conspicuous, because our own insufficiency and helplessness in ourselves is much more evident in our fallen and ruined state than it was before we were either sinful or miserable. We are more evidently dependent on God for holiness, because we are first sinful and utterly polluted, and afterward holy. So the producing of the effect can be perceived, and its coming from God is more obvious. If man had been holy from the start and always so, it would not be so evident that he did not have holiness necessarily, as an inseparable quality of human nature. So too we are more evidently dependent on free grace for the favor of God, for we are first justly the objects of his displeasure, and afterward are received into favor. We are more evidently dependent on God for happiness, being first miserable and afterward happy. It is more evidently free and without merit in us, because we are actually without any kind of excellence to merit it, if there could be any such thing as merit in a creature’s excellence. And we are not only without any true excellence, but are full of, and wholly defiled with, what is infinitely hateful. All our good is more evidently from God, because we are first naked and wholly without any good, and afterward enriched with all good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. We receive all of the #emph[power] of God. Man’s redemption is often spoken of as a work of wonderful power as well as grace. The great power of God appears in bringing a sinner from his low state, from the depths of sin and misery, to such an exalted state of holiness and happiness: “the surpassing greatness of His power to us who believe … in accordance with the working of His mighty strength …” (Eph 1:19).]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[We depend on God’s power through every step of our redemption. We depend on the power of God to convert us, and to give faith in Jesus Christ and the new nature. It is a work of creation: “if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation” (2 Cor 5:17); “we are … created in Christ Jesus” (Eph 2:10). The fallen creature cannot attain true holiness except by being created again: “and to put on the new self, created to be like God in true righteousness and holiness” (Eph 4:24). It is a raising from the dead: “… you#super[+] were raised with Him through your#super[+] faith in the power of God, who raised Him from the dead” (Col 2:12–13). Indeed, it is a more glorious work of power than mere creation, or raising a dead body to life, in that the effect attained is greater and more excellent. That holy and happy being and spiritual life which is reached in the work of conversion is a far greater and more glorious effect than mere being and life. And the state from which the change is made, such a death in sin, such total corruption of nature, and such depth of misery, is far more remote from the state attained than the mere death of nonexistence.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is by God’s power also that we are preserved in a state of grace: “who through faith are kept by God’s power for salvation” (1 Pet 1:5). As grace is at first from God, so it is continually from him and is maintained by him, just as the light in the atmosphere is from the sun all day long, as well as at first dawn, or at sunrise.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Men depend on the power of God for every exercise of grace, and for carrying on the work of grace in the heart: for subduing sin and corruption, increasing holy principles, and enabling them to bring forth fruit in good works. They depend on it for bringing grace at last to its perfection, in making the soul completely lovely in Christ’s glorious likeness and filling it with a satisfying joy and blessedness. And they depend on it for raising the body to life, and to such a perfect state that it will be fit to be a dwelling and instrument for a soul so perfected and blessed. These are the most glorious effects of the power of God that are seen in the whole course of God’s acts toward the creatures.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Man depended on the power of God in his first estate, but he depends on his power more now. He needs God’s power to do more things for him, and depends on a more wonderful exercise of his power. It was an effect of the power of God to make man holy at the first; but it is more remarkably so now, because there is a great deal of opposition and difficulty in the way. It is a more glorious effect of power to make holy what was so depraved and under the dominion of sin than to confer holiness on what had nothing of the contrary before. It is a more glorious work of power to rescue a soul out of the hands of the devil, and from the powers of darkness, and to bring it into a state of salvation, than to confer holiness where nothing held possession beforehand or stood in opposition: “When a strong man, fully armed, guards his house, his possessions are secure. But when someone stronger attacks and overpowers him, he takes away the armor in which the man trusted, and then he divides up his plunder” (Luke 11:21–22). So too it is a more glorious work of power to uphold a soul in a state of grace and holiness, and to carry it on until it is brought to glory, when there is so much sin remaining in the heart, resisting, and Satan with all his might opposing, than it would have been to keep man from falling at first, when Satan had nothing in man.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So we have shown how the redeemed depend on God for all their good, as they have all #emph[of] him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, they also depend on God for all, as they have all #emph[through] him. God is the medium of it, as well as its author and fountain. All that we have (wisdom, the pardon of sin, deliverance from hell, acceptance into God’s favor, grace and holiness, true comfort and happiness, eternal life and glory) we have from God by a Mediator; and this Mediator is God. On this Mediator we have an absolute dependence, as the one #emph[through] whom we receive all. So here is another way in which we depend on God for all good. God not only gives us the Mediator, and accepts his mediation, and by his power and grace bestows the things the Mediator purchased; he is himself the Mediator.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Our blessings are what we have by purchase. The purchase is made from God: the blessings are purchased from him, and God gives the purchaser. And not only so, but God is the purchaser. Indeed, God is both the purchaser and the price; for Christ, who is God, purchased these blessings for us by offering up himself as the price of our salvation. He purchased eternal life by the sacrifice of himself: “He offered up Himself” (Heb 7:27); and “He has appeared … to do away with sin by the sacrifice of Himself” (Heb 9:26). It is true that it was the human nature that was offered; but it was the same person as the divine, and therefore it was an infinite price. It was regarded as if God had been offered in sacrifice.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As we thus have our good #emph[through] God, we depend on God in a respect in which man in his first estate did not. Then man was to have eternal life through his own righteousness, so that he partly depended on what was in himself. For we depend on that #emph[through] which we have our good, as well as on that #emph[from] which we have it. And though the righteousness man then depended on was indeed from God, yet it was his own; it was inherent in himself, so that his dependence was not so immediately on God. But now the righteousness that we depend on is not in ourselves, but in God. We are saved through the righteousness of Christ: he “has become for us righteousness.” That is why he is prophesied of in Jer 23:6 under the name “The #smallcaps[Lord] Our Righteousness.” Since the righteousness by which we are justified is the righteousness of Christ, it is the righteousness of God: “so that in Him we might become the righteousness of God” (2 Cor 5:21).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So in redemption we have all things not only #emph[of] God, but #emph[by] and #emph[through] him: “yet for us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we in Him; and there is but one Lord, Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by Him” (1 Cor 8:6).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, the redeemed have all their good #emph[in] God. We not only have it #emph[of] him and #emph[through] him, but it consists #emph[in] him; he #emph[is] all our good.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The good of the redeemed is either objective or inherent. By their objective good I mean that object outside them in the possession and enjoyment of which they are happy. Their inherent good is that excellence or pleasure which is in the soul itself. With respect to both, the redeemed have all their good in God; or, which is the same thing, God himself is all their good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The redeemed have all their #emph[objective] good in God. God himself is the great good which redemption brings them to possess and enjoy. He is the highest good, and the sum of all the good which Christ purchased. God is the inheritance of the saints; he is the portion of their souls. God is their wealth and treasure, their food, their life, their dwelling place, their ornament and crown, and their everlasting honor and glory. They have no one in heaven but God. He is the great good which the redeemed are received into at death, and which they are to rise to at the end of the world. The #emph[Lord God] is the light of the heavenly Jerusalem. He is the “river of the water of life” that flows, and the “tree of life” that grows in the midst of the “paradise of God.” The glorious excellencies and beauty of God will be what forever engages the minds of the saints, and the love of God will be their everlasting feast. The redeemed will indeed enjoy other things: they will enjoy the angels, and will enjoy one another. But whatever delight and happiness they find in the angels, or in each other, or in anything else whatever, will be what is seen of God in them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The redeemed have all their #emph[inherent] good in God. Inherent good is of two kinds: it is either #emph[excellence] or #emph[pleasure.] The redeemed not only derive these from God, as caused by him, but have them in him. They have spiritual excellence and joy by a kind of participation in God. They are made excellent by an imparting of God’s excellence: God puts his own beauty, that is, his beautiful likeness, on their souls. They are made “partakers of the divine nature,” or of the moral image of God (2 Pet 1:4). They are holy by being made partakers of God’s holiness (Heb 12:10). The saints are beautiful and blessed by an imparting of God’s holiness and joy, as the moon and planets are bright by the sun’s light. The saint has spiritual joy and pleasure by a kind of outpouring of God on the soul. In these things the redeemed have communion with God; that is, they partake with him and of him.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The saints have both their spiritual excellence and their blessedness by the gift of the Holy Spirit, the Spirit of God, and by his dwelling in them. These are not only caused by the Holy Spirit, but are in the Holy Spirit as their principle. The Holy Spirit, becoming an inhabitant, is a vital principle in the soul. Acting in, on and with the soul, he becomes a fountain of true holiness and joy, as a spring is a fountain of water, by putting itself forth and pouring itself out. “But whoever drinks the water I give him will never thirst. Indeed, the water I give him will become in him a spring of water springing up to eternal life” (John 4:14). Compare John 7:38–39: “Whoever believes in Me, as the Scripture has said: ‘Rivers of living water will flow from within him.’ He was speaking about the Spirit, whom those who believed in Him were later to receive.” The sum of what Christ has purchased for us is that spring of water spoken of in the first of these places, and those rivers of living water spoken of in the second. And the sum of the blessings which the redeemed will receive in heaven is that “river of the water of life … flowing from the throne of God and of the Lamb” (Rev 22:1). This certainly signifies the same thing as those “rivers of living water” explained in John 7:38–39. It is elsewhere called the #emph[river of God’s pleasures.] In this consists the fullness of good which the saints receive from Christ. It is by partaking of the Holy Spirit that they have communion with Christ in his fullness. God has given the Spirit to him without measure; and they receive of his fullness, and grace for grace. This is the sum of the saints’ inheritance; and that is why the little of the Holy Spirit which believers have in this world is said to be “the earnest of their inheritance”: “He … placed His seal on us, and put His Spirit in our hearts” (2 Cor 1:22). And 2 Cor 5:5: “And God has prepared us for this very purpose and has given us the Spirit as an earnest of what is to come.” And Eph 1:13–14: “you#super[+] were sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise, who is the earnest of our inheritance until the redemption of the purchased possession.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The #emph[Holy Spirit] and #emph[good things] are spoken of in Scripture as the same, as if the Spirit of God imparted to the soul included all good things: “how much more will your#super[+] Father in heaven give good things to those who ask Him!” (Matt 7:11). In #emph[Luke] it is: “how much more will your#super[+] Father in heaven give the Holy Spirit to those who ask Him!” (Luke 11:13). This is the sum of the blessings that Christ died to obtain, and that are the subject of the gospel promises: “He became a curse for us … so that by faith we might receive the promise of the Spirit” (Gal 3:13–14). The Spirit of God is the great promise of the Father: “behold, I am sending the promise of My Father upon you#super[+]” (Luke 24:49). That is why the Spirit of God is called “the Spirit of promise” (Eph 1:13). Christ received this promised gift, and had it given into his hand, as soon as he had finished the work of our redemption, to bestow on all whom he had redeemed: “Exalted, then, to the right hand of God, He has received from the Father the promised Holy Spirit and has poured out what you#super[+] now see and hear” (Acts 2:33). So all the holiness and happiness of the redeemed is #emph[in] God. It is in the imparting, indwelling and acting of the Spirit of God. Holiness and happiness are in the fruit, here and hereafter, because #emph[God dwells in] them, #emph[and they in God.]]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thus it is God who has given us the Redeemer, and it is of him that our good is purchased; likewise it is God who is the Redeemer and the price; and it is God also who is the good purchased. So all that we have is #emph[of] God, and #emph[through] him, and #emph[in] him: “For #emph[of] Him and #emph[through] Him and to Him,” or in Him, “are all things” (Rom 11:36). The same word in the Greek that is here translated #emph[to him] is translated #emph[in him] in 1 Cor 8:6.]

II. God is glorified in the work of redemption by this means: by there being so great and universal a dependence of the redeemed on him.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Man has so much the greater occasion and obligation to take notice of and acknowledge God’s perfections and all-sufficiency. The greater the creature’s dependence on God’s perfections, and the greater his concern with them, the greater occasion he has to take notice of them. The greater concern anyone has with the power and grace of God, and the greater his dependence on them, the greater occasion he has to take notice of that power and grace. The greater and more immediate the dependence on the divine holiness, the greater the occasion to take notice of and acknowledge that. The greater and more absolute the dependence we have on the divine perfections, as belonging to each of the persons of the Trinity, the greater occasion we have to observe and acknowledge the divine glory of each of them. What we are most concerned with surely lies most in the way of our observation and notice; and this kind of concern with anything, namely dependence, especially tends to draw and bind our attention and observation. Things we do not much depend on are easy to neglect; but we can hardly do anything other than heed what we greatly depend on. Because our dependence on God and his perfections is so great, and in so many respects, he and his glory are set the more directly in our view, whichever way we turn our eyes.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[We have the greater occasion to take notice of God’s all-sufficiency when all our sufficiency is thus in every way of him. We have the more occasion to contemplate him as an infinite good, and as the fountain of all good. Such a dependence on God #emph[demonstrates] God’s all-sufficiency. The more the creature depends on God, the greater the creature’s emptiness in himself appears to be; and the greater the creature’s emptiness, the greater must be the fullness of the being who supplies him. Our having all #emph[of] God shows the fullness of his power and grace. Our having all #emph[through] him shows the fullness of his merit and worthiness. And our having all #emph[in] him demonstrates his fullness of beauty, love and happiness.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And because of the greatness of their dependence on God, the redeemed have not only so much the greater occasion, but also the greater obligation, to contemplate and acknowledge the glory and fullness of God. How unreasonable and ungrateful would we be if we did not acknowledge that sufficiency and glory on which we absolutely, immediately and universally depend?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. By this it is shown how great God’s glory is when considered comparatively, that is, compared with the creatures. By the creature’s being thus wholly and universally dependent on God, it appears that the creature is nothing, and that God is all. By this it appears that God is infinitely above us; that God’s strength, and wisdom, and holiness are infinitely greater than ours. However great and glorious the creature understands God to be, yet if he does not feel the difference between God and himself, so as to see that God’s glory is great compared with his own, he will not be disposed to give God the glory due to his name. If the creature in any respect sets himself on a level with God, or exalts himself to any competition with him, then however much he may understand that great honor and profound respect may belong to God from those who are more inferior and at a greater distance, he will not be so aware that it is due from himself. The more men exalt themselves, the less, surely, they will be disposed to exalt God. It is certainly something God aims at in the way he has ordered things in the matter of redemption (if we allow the Scriptures to be a revelation of God’s mind) that God should appear full, and man in himself empty; that God should appear all, and man nothing. It is God’s declared design that others should not #emph[glory in his presence,] which implies that it is his design to advance his own comparative glory. The more man #emph[glories in God’s presence,] the less glory is ascribed to God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. By its being so ordered that the creature should have so absolute and universal a dependence on God, provision is made that God should have our whole souls and be the object of our undivided regard. If we depended partly on God and partly on something else, man’s regard would be divided among the different things on which he depended. So it would be if we depended on God for only a part of our good, and on ourselves or some other being for another part; or if we had our good only #emph[from] God, but #emph[through] another who was not God, and #emph[in] something else distinct from both. Our hearts would then be divided between the good itself, and him #emph[from] whom, and him #emph[through] whom, we received it. But now there is no occasion for this, since God is not only the one #emph[from] or #emph[of] whom we have all good, but also the one #emph[through] whom we have it, and the one who is that good itself which we have from him and through him. So whatever there is to attract our regard, its tendency is always directly toward God; all unites in him as the center.]]

== Use

1\. Here we may observe the marvelous wisdom of God in the work of redemption. God has made man’s emptiness and misery, his low, lost and ruined state into which he has sunk by the fall, an occasion for the greater advancement of his own glory. He has done so in other ways, and particularly in this: that there is now a much more universal and evident dependence of man on God. Though God is pleased to lift man out of that dismal abyss of sin and woe into which he had fallen, and to exalt him exceedingly in excellence and honor, to a high pitch of glory and blessedness, yet the creature has nothing in any respect to glory in. All the glory clearly belongs to God; all is in a pure, most absolute and divine dependence on the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.

And each person of the Trinity is equally glorified in this work. There is an absolute dependence of the creature on every one of them for all: all is #emph[of] the Father, all #emph[through] the Son, and all #emph[in] the Holy Spirit. So God appears in the work of redemption as “all in all.” It is fitting that he who #emph[is, and there is no other,] should be the #emph[Alpha] and #emph[Omega,] the first and the last, the all and the only, in this work.

2\. Hence those doctrines and schemes of theology that are in any respect opposed to such an absolute and universal dependence on God detract from God’s glory, and thwart the design of the plan for our redemption. These are the schemes that put the creature in God’s place in any of the respects mentioned, that exalt man into the place of either Father, Son, or Holy Spirit in anything pertaining to our redemption. They are those that, however much they may allow a dependence of the redeemed on God, yet deny a dependence that is so absolute and universal; that acknowledge an entire dependence on God for some things, but not for others; that acknowledge that we depend on God for the gift and acceptance of a Redeemer, but deny so absolute a dependence on him for obtaining a share in the Redeemer; that acknowledge an absolute dependence on the Father for giving his Son, and on the Son for working out redemption, but not so entire a dependence on the Holy Spirit for conversion and for being in Christ, and so coming to a title to his benefits; that acknowledge a dependence on God for the means of grace, but not absolutely for the benefit and success of those means; that acknowledge a partial dependence on the power of God for obtaining and exercising holiness, but not a sole dependence on the unconstrained and sovereign grace of God; that acknowledge a dependence on the free grace of God for reception into his favor, so far as it is without any proper merit, but not as it is without being attracted or moved by any excellence; that acknowledge a partial dependence on Christ, as the one through whom we have life, as having purchased new terms of life, but still hold that the righteousness through which we have life is inherent in ourselves, as it was under the first covenant. And in whatever other way any scheme is inconsistent with our entire dependence on God for all, and in each of those ways of having all #emph[of] him, #emph[through] him, and #emph[in] him, it is contrary to the design and tenor of the gospel, and robs it of what God counts its luster and glory.

3\. Hence we may learn a reason why faith is the means by which we come to have a share in this redemption: for there is included in the nature of faith a sense and acknowledgment of this absolute dependence on God in this matter. It is very fitting that it should be required of all, in order for them to have the benefit of this redemption, that they should feel and acknowledge their dependence on God for it. It is by this means that God has designed to glorify himself in redemption; and it is fitting that God should at least have this glory from those who are the subjects of this redemption and have its benefit.

Faith is a sense of what is real in the work of redemption. And as we do in fact wholly depend on God, so the soul that believes entirely depends on God for all salvation, in its own sense and act. Faith abases men and exalts God; it gives all the glory of redemption to God alone. It is necessary to saving faith that man should be emptied of himself, that he should feel that he is “wretched, pitiful, poor, blind, and naked.” Humility is a great ingredient of true faith: he who truly receives redemption receives it “like a little child.” “Anyone who does not receive the kingdom of God like a little child will never enter it” (Mark 10:15). It is the delight of a believing soul to abase itself and exalt God alone. This is its language: “Not to us, O #smallcaps[Lord], not to us, but to Your name be the glory” (Ps 115:1).

4\. Let us be exhorted to exalt God alone, and to ascribe to him all the glory of redemption. Let us strive to obtain, and to increase in, a sense of our great dependence on God, to keep our eye on him alone, and to mortify a self-dependent and self-righteous disposition. Man is by nature exceedingly prone to exalt himself and depend on his own power or goodness, as though he were the one from whom he must expect happiness; and to look to enjoyments alien from God and his Spirit, as those in which happiness is to be found.

And this doctrine should teach us to exalt God alone, by trust and reliance and also by praise. “Let him who glories glory in the Lord.” Does any man hope that he is converted and sanctified, that his mind is endowed with true excellence and spiritual beauty, that his sins are forgiven, and that he is received into God’s favor and exalted to the honor and blessedness of being his child and an heir of eternal life? Let him give God all the glory, who alone makes him differ from the worst of men in this world, or the most miserable of the damned in hell. Does any man have much comfort and a strong hope of eternal life? Let his hope not lift him up, but dispose him all the more to abase himself, to reflect on his own exceeding unworthiness of such a favor, and to exalt God alone. Is any man eminent in holiness, and abundant in good works? Let him take nothing of the glory of it to himself, but ascribe it to him whose “workmanship” we are, “created in Christ Jesus to do good works.”
